Single-finger scroll was non-functional on Chromium for Android. Two-finger pinch-pan worked, desktop was unaffected.

## Changes

- **`touch-action: pan-y` on `html` + `body`** — without this, Chromium's compositor blocks scroll until it can verify no JS `preventDefault()` is pending. Especially problematic here since `body` carries a `transform` (page fade-in), which promotes it to a compositor layer.

- **Removed `will-change: transform` from `.hero-background`** — the full-viewport `position: fixed` background was being promoted to its own GPU compositor layer, which can cause touch hit-testing to misfire in Chromium mobile even with `pointer-events: none`. The element never transforms, so the hint was both wrong and harmful.

- **Removed `contain: strict` from `.hero-background`** — over-specified containment (`size layout paint style`) on a static decorative element; no benefit, potential scroll-container confusion.

```css
/* Before */
html { overflow-x: hidden; overscroll-behavior: none; }
body { ... overflow-x: hidden; overscroll-behavior: none; ... }

.hero-background {
    position: fixed; width: 100%; height: 100%;
    pointer-events: none;
    will-change: transform;   /* ← promotes compositor layer, breaks touch routing */
    contain: strict;          /* ← over-containment */
}

/* After */
html { overflow-x: hidden; overscroll-behavior: none; touch-action: pan-y; }
body { ... overflow-x: hidden; overscroll-behavior: none; touch-action: pan-y; ... }

.hero-background {
    position: fixed; width: 100%; height: 100%;
    pointer-events: none;
    /* will-change and contain removed */
}
```

